VT_TALK("sql/sql-aggregate", {
 "agg": "Aggregate functions take many rows and give back one answer. COUNT star counts rows, COUNT of a column counts known values, and COUNT DISTINCT counts different values. SUM adds, AVG finds the mean, and MIN and MAX find the smallest and biggest. group_concat joins many values into one piece of text. All of them except COUNT star skip NULLs. When a query has an aggregate but no GROUP BY, the whole table becomes one big group, so exactly one row comes back, even from an empty table. In that case COUNT gives zero and SUM gives NULL. Think of a teacher reading out only the class total and the class average, instead of every single mark.",
 "groupby": "GROUP BY is how you get one answer per group instead of one for the whole table. The engine puts rows that share a value into the same bucket, and then runs the aggregate once in each bucket, so every bucket becomes one row of the result. Watch the animation drop each product into its category bucket. There is one golden rule. Every column you show must either be a grouping column or sit inside an aggregate, because a group has many rows and the engine must know which value to show. Most engines refuse to break this rule, while SQLite allows it and picks a value from some row. Think of sorting laundry into piles by colour, then counting each pile.",
 "having": "WHERE and HAVING both filter, but they filter different things at different times. WHERE tests single rows before any groups are made, so it can never use COUNT or SUM. HAVING tests whole groups after they are made, so it can use aggregates freely. In the example, WHERE first throws away sold out products, then the groups form, then HAVING keeps only categories with at least two products left. Try the second block, it fails because WHERE cannot use COUNT. A neat trick is the FILTER clause or a SUM of CASE, which counts only some rows inside each group. Think of a school picking only students who passed, then keeping only classes with ten or more of them.",
 "order": "You write SELECT first, but the engine thinks about FROM first. The logical order is FROM, then WHERE, then GROUP BY, then HAVING, then SELECT, then DISTINCT, then ORDER BY, and finally LIMIT. Knowing this order explains the classic errors. WHERE cannot see a column alias, because the select list has not been worked out yet. ORDER BY can use the alias, because it comes after. HAVING can use aggregates, because the groups already exist. In the animation, change the parts and watch the table shrink at each stage. Real engines may do the work in a cleverer physical order, but the answer must match this logical order. Think of making tea, boil, add leaves, strain, then pour.",
 "levels": "For a child, GROUP BY makes piles, the aggregate counts each pile, and HAVING throws away the piles you do not want. At college level, engines group using sorting or hash tables, and features like ROLLUP add subtotals and a grand total in one query. At masters level, sums and counts can be split across many workers and joined later, and an average is just a sum and a count combined at the end, but a median cannot be split so easily. At research level, analytics systems use clever approximate counting to handle billions of rows quickly. Think of a census, where every district counts its people and the country simply adds the district totals."
});
